<!--
  Deliberate API breaks, declared rather than skipped.

  == The AudioSocket wire format ==

  Every entry in this group is a consequence of one fact: both AudioSocket implementations read a frame
  header Asterisk does not send. Measured against a real Asterisk 22.9.0 with the real res_audiosocket.so
  — the first bytes on the wire are `01 00 10` plus sixteen of UUID, so the header is one type byte and
  two of big-endian length, and `0x01` is the identification frame rather than audio. The values these
  suppressions cover described a protocol no Asterisk speaks, which is why no consumer can have
  depended on them for anything that worked.

  CP0011 (value changed): Uuid, Audio, Error and Hangup take the values res_audiosocket.h defines.
  CP0002 (member removed): Silence describes a frame kind Asterisk has no code for.

  == externalMedia gains a channelId parameter ==

  CP0002 (member removed): AriChannelsResource.CreateExternalMediaAsync at its old nine-parameter arity.
  The method is not deleted — it gained an optional `string? channelId` between `data` and its
  cancellation token — but optional parameters are compile-time sugar and ApiCompat matches a member by
  its full signature, so the nine-parameter member is genuinely absent from the assembly and a caller
  compiled against 2.5.3 does not bind to the ten-parameter one without a rebuild.

  The break is bought, not stumbled into. Asterisk keys an AudioSocket stream by the UUID the creator
  supplied in `data`, and mints the ARI channel id itself, so a consumer holding only a Channel.Id has
  no way to find its stream — `channelId` is the one ARI parameter that makes those two the same value.
  The alternative that breaks nothing, minting the uuid and passing it only as `data`, was measured
  working against a real Asterisk and rejected in the change proposal, because it fixes one activity and
  leaves the capability unfixed. What a consumer has to do is written down in
  docs/guides/externalmedia-channel-id-migration.md, per ADR-0028.

  This file is committed on purpose. ADR-0055 records that letting `pack` generate it on the build
  machine turns every difference into a suppression that never reaches the repository — which is
  skipping validation while it reports green. Each entry here was read before it was kept; the
  generated file was produced into a scratch path, compared entry by entry against the three breaks
  this change intends, and found to hold those and nothing else.

  These clear when PackageValidationBaselineVersion moves past the release that ships this change.
  Do not add an entry here to make a build pass; add it because the break is intended and written down.
-->
<Suppressions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0002</DiagnosticId>
    <Target>F:Verbara.Sdk.Ari.Audio.AudioFrameType.Silence</Target>
    <Left>lib/net10.0/Verbara.Sdk.Ari.dll</Left>
    <Right>lib/net10.0/Verbara.Sdk.Ari.dll</Right>
    <IsBaselineSuppression>true</IsBaselineSuppression>
